> First, I would like to say that I am somewhat new (a hundred or so
> deaths) to Nethack, and this may be something that all noobs
> experience, and I hope so: I find that most of my deaths are very
> sudden and completely unexpected. It is not that I am trying random
> things, but I am just either attacked, surrounded, or something else
> happens all of a sudden.

Don't let you get surrounded; use and retreat to corridors for that purpose.
Try to keep an escape route open. If you see something approaching, decide
whether to fight or flee. Using the up/down stairs will keep you safe from
monsters on the previous level, monsters don't follow you. But if you have
not solved your monster problems on the adjacent levels that won't help you
much. If you cannot flee, try writing in the dust the E-word ("Elbereth").
Some deaths are really unavoidable, so don't worry too much. Some deaths can
be prevented if you've obtained some resistances before; poison resistance
is a prominent example for a common early game killer.

> Also, (this may be a bad idea) under what circumstances should I ditch
> my pet? I have tried both keeping him and leaving him behind, and I
> find that I can survive by myself more conveniently and, for the most
> part, longer. I don't know if this is advisable, or even sane, so
> advise towards that would very well help me realize if I am just
> crazy.

For some fighter classes the pet might feel like an annoyance. Generally
it's good to keep your pets; they support you in fights and block a monster
from approaching to you in a corridor. Pets are very multifold and effective
tactical means. The starting pets grow up and get even tougher if you keep
them and if they fight for you. You need some pet management, though; if you
want to rely on pets try to keep them close. Especially if you're lacking
food in the early game you shouldn't wait too long for your pet to follow
you. You need a bit experience to be able to optimize your per management,
but (as in so many aspects of Nethack) there's quite a range for handling
pets effectively.

